<p>Getting a denial letter after a storm, leak, or fire is discouraging, especially when the damage is right in front of you. A <strong>denied insurance claim in San Marcos</strong> is not always the end of the claim, though. Many denials rest on a short inspection, a disputed cause of loss, missing paperwork, or a broad reading of an exclusion. As a San Marcos public adjuster, we help homeowners and business owners understand why the claim was denied, test the insurer's reasoning against the policy and the evidence, and decide whether there is a reasonable basis to ask for reconsideration.</p>

<h2>Common Reasons Claims Are Denied in San Marcos</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Denial reason</th><th>What the insurer may be saying</th><th>Evidence that may help</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Wear and tear / age</td><td>Roof or building damage is from deterioration, not a covered event</td><td>Storm reports for the date, hail marks on soft metals, photos showing fresh damage</td></tr>
<tr><td>No storm damage found</td><td>The inspection did not identify hail or wind damage</td><td>An independent inspection with test squares, close-up photos, and measurements</td></tr>
<tr><td>Long-term leak or seepage</td><td>Water damage happened over weeks or months</td><td>Plumber's report on the failure, timeline of discovery, moisture readings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood exclusion</td><td>Water came from surface water or rising water, not covered under the policy</td><td>Evidence the water entered through a storm-created opening or from plumbing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Late notice</td><td>The claim was reported too long after the loss</td><td>When the damage was discovered and why it was not visible earlier</td></tr>
<tr><td>Below deductible</td><td>The estimated damage is less than the deductible</td><td>A complete line-item estimate showing scope the insurer missed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Excluded cause</td><td>Mold, settling, foundation movement, or pests</td><td>Policy wording on exceptions and any ensuing-loss language</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Why Causation Disputes Are Common in Hays County</h2>
<p>Hays County gets frequent spring hail. NOAA's Storm Events Database lists 180 hail reports in the county since 1967, with the heaviest concentration in April and May. Because many roofs have been through more than one storm, insurers sometimes attribute damage to age or to an earlier event that falls outside the policy period. That makes the date and cause of damage central to many denials.</p>
<p>Storm records can help. On May 9, 2024, for example, NOAA logged <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181388" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3-inch hail at San Marcos Academy</a> and <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181409" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2.5-inch hail near the outlet mall</a>. A report like that does not prove your roof was damaged, but paired with photos of fresh impacts it can help show when and how damage occurred. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> tool and <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page are good starting points.</p>
<p>Water claims raise a different causation question. Texas homeowners policies generally exclude flood, and many exclude repeated or long-term seepage. Whether a loss counts as a sudden discharge, storm-driven rain through a damaged roof, or surface water can decide the claim. See <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a>.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/homeowner-reviewing-denial-letter.webp" alt="Worried homeowner reading a letter at a desk with a laptop and calculator" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Read the denial letter closely; it should name the reasons and the policy language the insurer relied on.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How to Read a Denial Letter</h2>
<p>Under the Texas Prompt Payment of Claims Act (<a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Insurance Code Chapter 542</a>), an insurer that rejects a claim must say so in writing and give its reasons. When you get the letter, look for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The specific reason</strong> for the denial, not just a general statement.</li>
<li><strong>The policy provisions quoted</strong>, including the exclusion or condition relied on.</li>
<li><strong>Whether the denial is full or partial.</strong> Some letters deny one part, such as the interior, and accept another.</li>
<li><strong>What the decision was based on</strong>: the field adjuster's inspection, an engineer's report, photos, or a recorded statement.</li>
<li><strong>Any deadlines or next steps</strong> the insurer mentions.</li>
</ul>
<p>Request a full copy of your policy and the inspection photos and reports if you do not already have them. These are the documents any response will need to address.</p>

