<h2>How a Public Adjuster Can Help After a Denial</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Policy and letter review.</strong> We compare the stated reason against the actual policy language, including exceptions to exclusions and ensuing-loss wording.</li>
<li><strong>Independent inspection.</strong> We document the damage with photos, measurements, and, where helpful, Matterport 3D imaging.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence of cause and date.</strong> We gather weather data, repair records, and other evidence tied to the reported date of loss.</li>
<li><strong>Written reconsideration request.</strong> We present the new documentation to the insurer and follow up in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Next options.</strong> If part of the claim is accepted and the dispute is about value, <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> may be worth discussing. If the dispute is about coverage itself, a Texas attorney is the right resource.</li>
</ol>
<p>Not every denial should be challenged. Sometimes the policy clearly excludes the loss, and an honest review should say so. When there is a real basis to contest the decision, organized evidence gives the insurer something specific to reconsider. If the insurer accepted the claim but paid too little, see <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> instead.</p>

<h2>Full Denial vs. Partial Denial</h2>
<p>Not every denial letter rejects the whole claim. A partial denial may accept the roof but exclude interior water damage, or accept the dwelling but deny a fence or detached workshop. Partial denials are easy to overlook because a check arrives with the letter, and the policyholder assumes the matter is settled. Read the letter alongside the estimate to see exactly which items were excluded and why. A partial denial may raise both a coverage question for the excluded items and a value question for the accepted ones, and those questions are handled differently. A San Marcos public adjuster can help separate the two so each gets the right response.</p>

<h2>Steps to Take Right Now</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep the denial letter and every email and letter about the claim.</li>
<li>Do not throw away damaged materials or make permanent repairs before the damage is documented, unless needed to prevent further damage.</li>
<li>Write down your timeline: when the damage happened, when you noticed it, and when you reported it.</li>
<li>Gather photos from before and after the event, including real estate listing photos or older inspection reports.</li>
<li>Check your policy for deadlines and consult a Texas attorney about legal ones.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our article on <a href="/what-to-do-if-your-claim-was-denied-in-san-marcos/">what to do if your claim was denied in San Marcos</a> walks through these steps in more detail.</p>

